faq:
  - q: How do I know my child needs to see a psychologist?
    a: If the difficulty has lasted a few weeks, affects daily life, and what you have tried at home is not helping, a conversation is worthwhile. A sudden drop in school performance, changes in sleep or appetite, social withdrawal, frequent outbursts or unexplained physical complaints are common signs.
  - q: How many sessions does child therapy take?
    a: It varies from child to child. Work focused on one specific difficulty often runs somewhere between 8 and 12 sessions; developmental or long-standing difficulties can take longer. Progress is reviewed with you at regular intervals.
  - q: How do I tell my child we are seeing a psychologist?
    a: 'Plain and honest language works: "There is someone who talks with children and understands them. We will go together." Avoid surprises, and avoid framing it as a doctor or an injection. Leave room for questions; saying "I do not know" is also an answer.'
  - q: Do we need a diagnosis to see a child psychologist?
    a: No. A large share of the families who come have no diagnosis. A parent not being sure about something is reason enough. After an assessment, a single parent session is sometimes all that is needed.
  - q: Are online sessions suitable for children?
    a: For parent sessions and for work with adolescents, online sessions are often a good fit. With younger children, play-based work goes further in person. We can look at which one suits your child together.
  - q: Does the same process apply to adolescents?
    a: The approach changes. With an adolescent, the confidential space is wider, parent sessions become less frequent, and the young person's own goals shape the work. We have a separate area of practice for adolescence.
