      - baslik: Detailed assessment where needed
        aciklama: Developmental or attention-focused assessment tools
      - baslik: Regular sessions and parent reviews
        aciklama: The family stays in the process while the work with the child continues
      - baslik: Working with others when needed
        aciklama: With the school or a physician, to the extent the family agrees
  - type: metin
    baslik: When should you see a child psychologist?
    metin: |-
      If a behaviour has lasted a few weeks, affects your child's daily life — school, sleep, friendships, eating — and what you have tried at home is not helping, that is reason enough to talk. You do not need to wait for a crisis.

      It is more common than most families assume. A meta-analysis combining 27 studies across 41 countries (Polanczyk et al., 2015) reports that around 13.4% of children and adolescents meet criteria for a mental disorder. The World Health Organization describes a similar picture in one in seven young people aged 10 to 19. This is not the result of a mistake a family made; it is a common part of childhood.
